Subject: Re: [stan-dev] beta_lccdf(1 | a, b); log1p(-1); log1m(1); log(0) return -inf or throw overflow exception?
Message-ID: <f1iEHUTcut0.TD1dxP7mAwAJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <f1iEHUTcut0.8ul6mWnlAwAJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
References: <f1iEHUTcut0.Od9z_5rSAwAJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com> <f1iEHUTcut0.8ul6mWnlAwAJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/f1iEHUTcut0/m/TD1dxP7mAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

-infinity isn't totally useless:

  exp(-infinity) = 0

  -infinity < x for x finite, or x = +infinity

I think 1 / infinity = 0, too, but I'm not 100% sure.

But it'll almost certainly break derivatives when
you get 0 / 0 evaluating a derivative of exp(log(x))
for x = 0.

- Bob

